{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a1cbedccb8a2a8e2","type":"tool_result","content":"skills/epic-decompose/prompts/revise-agent.md:52:- **Splitting an epic (any reason) — wire the new epic's dependencies per the DAG construction rules; relatedness is not a dependency.** A sub-deliverable split within the same repo and area is parallel work (Rule 15): add no edge between the halves. Add an edge only when one half's code builds against an artifact the other half produces (API, CRD, library — Rule 13) or a construction rule explicitly creates one. Enforcement or verification coupling — one half lints, checks, or validates the other half's output — is an acceptance criterion on the verifying epic, never a `dependencies` edge.\nskills/epic-decompose/prompts/review-agent.md:76:Check: Trace the dependency graph. Verify each edge against the DAG construction rules (boundary rules 1-3, investigation edges 4-5, implementation type ordering 6-12, implementation edges 13-16, external dependency edges 17-19, generation rules 20-23, AC rules 24-26). Check that parallel-eligible work (different repos, no shared artifacts) is not unnecessarily serialized. **Implementation-type parallelism (rules 7, 8, 10) — enumerate and check every pair:** epics whose `implementation_type` pairing is mandated parallel must NOT have a blocking edge between them in either direction. Check **every** `konflux-onboarding` epic against **every** general-implementation epic (a decomposition often has more than one general epic — e.g. a component controller AND an OLM/CSV packaging epic; check all of them, not just the most obvious one). Specifically, a `konflux-onboarding → general implementation` edge (rule 10), `license-validation → repo-onboarding` (rule 7), or `license-validation → konflux-onboarding` (rule 8) is a **critical** issue — score this criterion 0. The rule covers ANY general implementation epic, whatever its component or deliverable — including stack-wide assembly, packaging, or evidence epics that consume the pipeline's outputs; \"the dependent epic is not the onboarded component's own implementation\" is not an exemption. The only permitted coupling between a `konflux-onboarding` epic and a general implementation epic is the Rule 25 \"build pipeline green\" acceptance criterion (or an equivalent AC on the consuming epic), never a `dependencies`/DAG edge. Do not accept a rationale that the code \"needs the pipeline first.\" **In particular, an edge justified only by an image digest / SHA / `RELATED_IMAGE` / endpoint URL — or by any other build-pipeline output consumed as CONTENT (signed attestations, SBOMs, provenance, scan results) — is a configuration reference (Rule 13), which must be an AC gate, not a `dependencies` edge — flag it as critical, do not accept it as an \"artifact dependency.\"** Symmetrically, do not report the absence of such an edge as a missing data/artifact dependency in the cross-check below: a build-pipeline output consumed as content is an AC gate, and its absence from the DAG is the rule-compliant shape. **Conditional branch files (`{ID}-BRANCH-<letter>-E*.md`), when present:** each MUST set both `branch` (the outcome letter, matching the filename) and `gated_by` (the main-plan Investigation `epic_id` that selects it) — a branch file missing either is a **critical** issue because the pipeline silently drops it. And no main-plan epic may list a branch epic in its `dependencies`: a main-plan epic depending on a `-BRANCH-` node (e.g. main-plan `E004` → `BRANCH-A-E003`) references an epic that is never created as an issue, a **critical** issue — work that hinges on the outcome must depend on the Investigation epic itself, and work that hinges on a specific branch belongs inside that branch. Note: Rule 11 edges (all implementations → `docs-authoring`) are valid DAG edges but do not trigger priority inheritance — do not flag them as unjustified serialization or priority inheritance violations. Verify critical path length against strategy size heuristics (S: 1-2, M standard: 3-4, M with new component: 4-5, L: 5-7). **Cross-check consistency**: The DAG diagram convention is `graph TD` with arrows from dependency to dependent (`E001 --> E003` means \"E001 must complete before E003 can start\"). Verify that every edge in the decomposition summary DAG diagram has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter, and vice versa. If arrows are drawn in the opposite direction (dependent → dependency), flag as a critical issue — the diagram is misleading even if frontmatter is correct. Any mismatch (edge in diagram but not in frontmatter, or frontmatter dependency referencing a nonexistent epic) is a critical issue — score 0. **Cross-check completeness**: also scan epic content (scope, ACs, descriptions) for data/artifact dependencies not captured in the DAG — e.g., an epic that consumes a schema, image, or API produced by another epic but has no edge to it. A missing edge discoverable from epic content is a critical issue even if the diagram and frontmatter are consistent with each other.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b8e084ffbbadeac","agent_id":"aaa91a67b57736f0a","session_id":"d3f3ba1a-747f-4186-8293-bb365a6cbf67","uuid":"37976b34-9dcc-4a62-b731-e31e54c15a61","timestamp":"2026-10-23T17:55:27.839Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a1cbedccb8a2a8e2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
