{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b46886ddf05834c4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014 (Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability)\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment for Llama Stack | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | API stability guarantee and tested upgrade path | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | GA quality gap remediation | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | Production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | TrustyAI guardrails as supported integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 | GA documentation uplift with TP migration notes | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n23\t\n24\tComponent assignments are verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`: Llama Stack (E001–E004), TrustyAI (E005), Dashboard (E006), Documentation (E007). E005's work spans TrustyAI and the Llama Stack distribution surface; the single most-relevant canonical component is TrustyAI (the integration being productized is TrustyAI's guardrails service, owned by the TrustyAI team). E007 documents the Llama Stack GA graduation; it is assigned to the Documentation component with the Llama Stack owning team because the strategy names no docs team (see Health Warnings).\n25\t\n26\t## Dependency DAG\n27\t\n28\t```mermaid\n29\tgraph TD\n30\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 GA-readiness assessment] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 API stability]\n31\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 quality gap remediation]\n32\t    E001 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 support posture]\n33\t    E003 --> E004\n34\t    E002 --> E007[RHAISTRAT-9014-E007 docs uplift]\n35\t    E003 --> E007\n36\t    E004 --> E007\n37\t    E005[RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 TrustyAI guardrails] --> E007\n38\t    E006[RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 Dashboard default-on] --> E007\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\t## DAG Justification\n42\t\n43\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n44\t|------|------|-----------|\n45\t| E001 → E002 | 3 (Investigation gating) | The assessment inventories the API-stability gaps; the strategy's open question states the size of the stability work is an estimate until it lands. True gate: `rewrite` — the assessed gap inventory changes what the stability work does. |\n46\t| E001 → E003 | 3 (Investigation gating) | E003's scope is literally the assessment's output (the remediation of inventoried gaps). True gate: `rewrite` — the epic's scope is defined by the assessment outcome. |\n47\t| E001 → E004 | 3 (Investigation gating) | The supported configuration matrix and SLO targets incorporate the assessed scale limits and error-handling findings. True gate: `rewrite`. |\n48\t| E003 → E004 | 13 (artifact dependency) | The supported configuration matrix incorporates the scale limits characterized by the quality remediation work — E004 consumes a work product of E003, so the matrix cannot be finalized before the limits are characterized. Not a configuration reference or build-pipeline output consumed as content (the AC-gate carve-out does not apply): the characterization is a sibling implementation epic's deliverable, so the epics are serial. |\n49\t| (none) → E005 | 14 (parallel) | Different team and repos (TrustyAI); HLR-4 is a fixed deliverable already in GA scope — the assessment's enumerated gap areas (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling) do not change whether this integration work exists. Coupling to the versioned API surface is expressed as an acceptance criterion on E005, not an edge. |\n50\t| (none) → E006 | 14 (parallel) | Different team and repo (Dashboard); HLR-5 is a fixed deliverable (remove TP opt-in gate for entitled clusters) independent of the Llama Stack hardening work. Release-time sequencing (default-on ships with GA) is release coordination, not a build dependency. |\n51\t| E002, E003, E004, E005, E006 → E007 | 11 (docs last) | `docs-authoring` is blocked by ALL Implementation epics — docs describe what was built, including the tested upgrade path (migration notes), support posture, guardrails integration, and default Dashboard enablement. |\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n54\t\n55\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n56\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n57\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001, E003 | E001 directly delivers the assessment; E003 closes the gaps it inventories. Together they deliver \"inventory the gaps … producing the definitive GA scope\" and the remediation that closes them. |\n58\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases. |\n59\t| HLR-3 | P0 | E004 | SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix. |\n60\t| HLR-4 | P1 | E005 | Supported, documented TrustyAI guardrails integration. E005 is the single authoring location for the integration documentation (same work stream, same surface; not split into the docs epic); E007 links to and technically reviews that content rather than re-authoring it. |\n61\t| HLR-5 | P1 | E006 | Dashboard default enablement for entitled clusters. |\n62\t| HLR-6 | P2 | E007 | GA-standard docs uplift with TP migration notes. |\n63\t\n64\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. Each HLR maps to a distinct deliverable on a distinct surface, so no priority-split (bundling) analysis is required — no epic carries HLRs at mixed priority levels, except as follows: E001 carries only HLR-1 (P0); E003 carries the remediation half of HLR-1 (P0). The docs epic's priority is P0 by the `docs-authoring` priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0), not by inheritance from HLR-6's P2 label; per Rule 11 its incoming edges do not push priority upstream, so E005/E006 remaining P1 is expected and is not an inversion.\n65\t\n66\t## Health Warnings\n67\t\n68\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements, and no Technical Approach / Staff Engineer Input. Teams were inferred from `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/*.md` owning teams (RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI, RHOAI Dashboard). Epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLR text. If actual team assignments differ, epic `team` fields need correction before submission.\n69\t- **Docs epic team assumption**: E007 is assigned to the Documentation component with team RHOAI GenAI (the component's owning team) because the strategy names no docs team. Confirm the actual docs owner.\n70\t- **Scope estimate until assessment lands**: per the strategy's open question, the size of E002/E003 is an estimate until E001 completes. This is managed by gating (E001 → E002/E003/E004), not by padding scope.\n71\t- **Re-split triggers (overage management)**: E002 (API versioning + deprecation policy + upgrade-path testing from two TP releases) and E003 (assessed-gap remediation) are each plausible candidates to exceed one sprint. They are kept as single epics because each is one logical change over one surface — splitting would duplicate the same audit or fragment the stability commitment. Re-split E002 if the assessed API-stability gap inventory exceeds an estimated sprint of effort (split deprecation-policy publication from upgrade-path test execution). Re-split E003 into per-gap epics if the assessment's remediation inventory exceeds a sprint of effort or spans architecturally distinct sub-systems.\n72\t- **Priority note on E005/E006**: both are P1 (their HLRs are P1) yet block the P0 docs epic. This is the expected consequence of Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) plus the docs-authoring priority exception — it is not an unanalyzed inversion.\n73\t\n74\t## Tiered Delivery\n75\t\n76\tNot applicable. The strategy has no external dependency with uncertain timing: all three components are in the platform (architecture context files exist for each), and all work is internal to the owning teams. The only uncertainty (GA scope size) is resolved by E001 and handled via gating, not tiered delivery.\n77\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a956c3fa5fc996c3","agent_id":"ae91568166fe76520","session_id":"d3f3ba1a-747f-4186-8293-bb365a6cbf67","uuid":"dab5da7a-a7fd-4525-a405-0aec9fc4ce11","timestamp":"2026-10-23T17:56:54.613Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b46886ddf05834c4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
