ation — usage, configuration, and support boundaries — to the level\nrequired for a supported GA integration\", with a matching AC). Content authoring and\ncode implementation have different review criteria and belong in separate epics even\nwhen owned by the same team. The decomposition's single-authoring-location rationale\nsolves the duplication risk against E007 but does so by placing the authoring inside the\ncode epic — the correct shape is the inverse: E005 keeps the ungating and validation\ncode work, and the integration documentation authoring moves to the docs-authoring epic\n(E007 owning authorship with E005 providing technical input, or a separate docs-content\nepic). This is a major issue; the criterion scores 0 per the major-issue rule.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (the gap inventory / definitive GA scope)\nchanges downstream epic scope, and all three gated epics declare `gated_by: E001` with\n`gate_failure_impact` carrying both `action: rewrite` and a concrete\n`fallback_approach`. Every `gated_by` target (E001) appears in the gated epic's direct\n`dependencies` list. E004's dual dependencies (E001 gated, E003 artifact) are correctly\nseparated into gate vs. scheduling dependency. E002–E006/E007 are Implementations that\neach produce concrete artifacts (versioned surface + policy, remediated gaps, published\nposture, supported integration, shipped default, published docs). E005 and E006\ncorrectly have no `gated_by` — they depend on no Investigation.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout: E001 carries `investigation_signals`\n(5-signal rubric); E002–E007 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no cross-type\ncontamination. Frontmatter values match the rationale files, and the rationales are\nsubstantive and calibrated against the strategy content — e.g., E002/E003\n`open_questions: -1` reflecting the strategy's stated open question, E005/E006\n`open