{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-980c49fdadfccae2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tscore: 12\n4\tpass: false\n5\trecommendation: revise\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: major\n8\t  criterion: Epic Boundaries\n9\t  description: 'E005 bundles code implementation (promote TrustyAI guardrails integration\n10\t    from experimental flag to supported, plus validation) with content authoring (integration\n11\t    documentation: usage, configuration, support boundaries). These are different\n12\t    kinds of work with different review criteria and must be separate epics even when\n13\t    owned by the same team. Correction: keep E005 for the ungating and validation\n14\t    code work, and move the integration documentation authoring into the docs-authoring\n15\t    epic (E007 owning authorship with E005 providing technical input) or a separate\n16\t    docs-content epic; update the E007 scope/ACs and the HLR-4 traceability row accordingly.'\n17\terror: null\n18\t---\n19\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9014\n20\t\n21\t## Review Summary\n22\t\n23\tScore: 12/14 — fail\n24\tRecommendation: revise\n25\t\n26\tcontract validator: OK (all mechanical checks passed: epic body sections, canonical\n27\tcomponent names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, implementation-type\n28\tparallelism). Triage check: `triage: null` (full decomposition), so the 7-epic output is\n29\texpected and not penalized.\n30\t\n31\tThe decomposition is coherent and well-justified on six of seven dimensions. It fails on\n32\tone structural boundary defect: E005 bundles code implementation with content authoring,\n33\twhich forces Epic Boundaries to 0 and triggers the auto-fail rule.\n34\t\n35\t## Criterion Details\n36\t\n37\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n38\tAll six HLRs map to at least one epic, and the traceability matrix is present and\n39\tcomplete: HLR-1 → E001/E003 (assessment half + remediation half, both P0, split\n40\treasoning recorded in the matrix row), HLR-2 → E002, HLR-3 → E004, HLR-4 → E005,\n41\tHLR-5 → E006, HLR-6 → E007. The P2 HLR-6 is covered rather than deferred, which exceeds\n42\tthe bar. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0) gates all P0 Llama Stack work;\n43\tE003 (P0) blocks E004 (P0); E005/E006 (P1) block only the docs-authoring epic E007,\n44\twhich is exempt from priority inheritance, and E007's P0 priority is correctly derived\n45\tfrom the strategy's Critical priority via the docs-authoring exception — this is\n46\tdocumented in both the summary and E007's HLR Traceability. No epic carries HLRs at\n47\tmixed priority levels, so no bundling/split analysis is required, and the summary\n48\tsays so explicitly.\n49\t\n50\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n51\tThe mermaid diagram (dependency → dependent arrows) matches every epic's frontmatter\n52\t`dependencies` exactly, in both directions: E001→{E002,E003,E004}, E003→E004,\n53\t{E002,E003,E004,E005,E006}→E007. No dangling references, no cycles. Rule 11 docs-last is\n54\tsatisfied: E007 (docs-authoring) is blocked by all five Implementation epics. The\n55\tE003→E004 edge is a genuine artifact dependency, not a configuration reference: E003's\n56\tdeliverable is the characterized scale limits that E004's supported configuration matrix\n57\tconsumes (E003's AC explicitly defines the handoff), and the DAG justification table\n58\taddresses the AC-gate carve-out directly. E005 and E006 correctly have no edges to any\n59\timplementation epic — E005's coupling to the versioned API surface is expressed as an\n60\tAC, which is the rule-compliant shape for a consumed surface reference. Critical path\n61\tlength 4 (E001→E003→E004→E007) is appropriate for a medium strategy. No conditional\n62\tbranch files exist, so the branch-file rules do not apply.\n63\t\n64\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n65\tComponent/team tuples are clean (Llama Stack/RHOAI GenAI for E001–E004, TrustyAI/TrustyAI,\n66\tDashboard/RHOAI Dashboard, Documentation/RHOAI GenAI), all components are canonical per\n67\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`, and E005's TrustyAI+Llama Stack span is reduced to the\n68\tsingle most-relevant canonical component with recorded rationale. E002/E003 size risk is\n69\thandled correctly: the summary records concrete re-split triggers in Health Warnings,\n70\twhich is the managed watch-item shape, not a defect. No scope duplication between\n71\tsiblings — the E002 (API-stability gaps) / E003 (other quality gaps) split follows the\n72\tassessment's gap-area assignment, and E005/E007 docs ownership is explicitly\n73\tde-duplicated.\n74\t\n75\tHowever, E005 bundles two different kinds of work product in one epic: code\n76\timplementation (moving the TrustyAI guardrails integration from experimental/opt-in\n77\tgating to supported status, plus validation work) and content authoring (scope item:\n78\t\"Document the integration — usage, configuration, and support boundaries — to the level\n79\trequired for a supported GA integration\", with a matching AC). Content authoring and\n80\tcode implementation have different review criteria and belong in separate epics even\n81\twhen owned by the same team. The decomposition's single-authoring-location rationale\n82\tsolves the duplication risk against E007 but does so by placing the authoring inside the\n83\tcode epic — the correct shape is the inverse: E005 keeps the ungating and validation\n84\tcode work, and the integration documentation authoring moves to the docs-authoring epic\n85\t(E007 owning authorship with E005 providing technical input, or a separate docs-content\n86\tepic). This is a major issue; the criterion scores 0 per the major-issue rule.\n87\t\n88\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n89\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (the gap inventory / definitive GA scope)\n90\tchanges downstream epic scope, and all three gated epics declare `gated_by: E001` with\n91\t`gate_failure_impact` carrying both `action: rewrite` and a concrete\n92\t`fallback_approach`. Every `gated_by` target (E001) appears in the gated epic's direct\n93\t`dependencies` list. E004's dual dependencies (E001 gated, E003 artifact) are correctly\n94\tseparated into gate vs. scheduling dependency. E002–E006/E007 are Implementations that\n95\teach produce concrete artifacts (versioned surface + policy, remediated gaps, published\n96\tposture, supported integration, shipped default, published docs). E005 and E006\n97\tcorrectly have no `gated_by` — they depend on no Investigation.\n98\t\n99\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n100\tType-appropriate signal sets are used throughout: E001 carries `investigation_signals`\n101\t(5-signal rubric); E002–E007 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no cross-type\n102\tcontamination. Frontmatter values match the rationale files, and the rationales are\n103\tsubstantive and calibrated against the strategy content — e.g., E002/E003\n104\t`open_questions: -1` reflecting the strategy's stated open question, E005/E006\n105\t`open_questions: +1` reflecting their fixed-deliverable nature, and E001's\n106\t`human_judgment_required: -1` reflecting stakeholder acceptance of the GA scope.\n107\t`architecture_claims: +1` rationales cite the specific architecture-context files\n108\t(llama-stack.md, trustyai.md, dashboard.md), which exist in\n109\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/` and corroborate the claimed\n110\tmaturity states and surfaces. No arithmetic or threshold checks performed (computed by\n111\tthe pipeline).\n112\t\n113\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n114\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLR text. Rule-mandated ACs are present:\n115\tE006 (the default-flip change) has the rollback AC (\"can be disabled through Dashboard\n116\tconfiguration without code rollback\"), and E007 (docs-authoring) has the technical\n117\treview AC plus consistency ACs against the implemented behavior. E005's validation\n118\tagainst the versioned API surface is correctly expressed as an AC rather than a DAG\n119\tedge. No soft-ACs found: the stakeholder-approval ACs on E001/E004 are conditions on\n120\teach epic's own deliverable (an accepted/approved document), not externally-controlled\n121\tavailability or schedule preconditions. No epic body references sibling epics by bare\n122\tdraft ID — cross-epic references use descriptive names (\"the guardrails work\", \"the GA\n123\tdocumentation uplift\").\n124\t\n125\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n126\tThe full strategy scope is covered: all HLRs mapped, the problem statement's four gap\n127\tdimensions (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling) appear in E001's\n128\tscope and are carried into E002/E003/E004, and out-of-scope constraints are respected\n129\t(E003 remediates assessed gaps only — no new feature development). The strategy's open\n130\tquestion (scope size unknown until assessment) is carried forward as a Health Warning\n131\tand structurally managed by gating. Strategy-level context that is missing from the\n132\tstrategy itself (no Impacted Teams, no Dependencies table, no NFRs) is flagged in Health\n133\tWarnings with the inference basis recorded rather than silently dropped. The docs epic\n134\texists for the documentation requirement (HLR-6). No conditional branches are needed —\n135\tthe summary's Tiered Delivery section correctly explains why (all components\n136\tin-platform, no external timing dependency).\n137\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aa67abe0817f95a7","agent_id":"a946d84bc05ac2c99","session_id":"d3f3ba1a-747f-4186-8293-bb365a6cbf67","uuid":"bf1fa00b-0c61-4754-9167-cbaec8edbad5","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:00:07.647Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-980c49fdadfccae2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Second revise RHAISTRAT-9014"}
